an10.53:5.1 | S: If a mendicant isn’t skilled in encompassing another’s mind, then they should train themselves: ‘I will be skilled in encompassing my own mind.’ | T: “Bhikkhus, a bhikkhu who is not skilled in the ways of others’ minds should train: ‘I will be skilled in the ways of my own mind.’
an10.53:5.2 | P: evañhi vo, bhikkhave, sikkhitabbaṁ. | T: (none)
an10.53:6.1 | S: And how is a mendicant skilled in encompassing their own mind? | T: (none)
an10.53:6.2 | S: Suppose there was a woman or man who was young, youthful, and fond of adornments, and they check their own reflection in a clean bright mirror or a clear bowl of water. If they see any dirt or blemish there, they’d try to remove it. | T: (none)
an10.53:6.3 | S: But if they don’t see any dirt or blemish there, they’re happy with that, as they’ve got all they wished for: | T: (none)
an10.53:6.4 | S: ‘How fortunate that I’m clean!’ | T: … [97–98] …
an10.53:6.5 | S: In the same way, checking is very helpful for a mendicant’s skillful qualities. | T: (none)
an10.53:6.6 | S: ‘Am I often covetous or not? Am I often malicious or not? Am I often overcome with dullness and drowsiness or not? Am I often restless or not? Am I often doubtful or not? Am I often irritable or not? Am I often corrupted in mind or not? Am I often disturbed in body or not? Am I often energetic or not? Am I often immersed in samādhi or not?’ | T: (none)
an10.53:7.1 | S: Suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows this: | T: as in AN10.51 down to:
an10.53:7.2 | S: ‘I am often covetous, malicious, overcome with dullness and drowsiness, restless, doubtful, irritable, corrupted in mind, disturbed in body, lazy, and not immersed in samādhi.’ In order to give up those bad, unskillful qualities, they should apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: (none)
an10.53:7.3 | S: Suppose your clothes or head were on fire. In order to extinguish it, you’d apply extraordinary enthusiasm, effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness. | T: (none)
an10.53:7.4 | P: Tasseva celassa vā sīsassa vā nibbāpanāya adhimattaṁ chandañca vāyāmañca ussāhañca ussoḷhiñca appaṭivāniñca satiñca sampajaññañca kareyya; | T: (none)
an10.53:7.5 | S: In the same way, in order to give up those bad, unskillful qualities, that mendicant should apply extraordinary enthusiasm … | T: …
an10.53:8.1 | S: But suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows this: | T: But if, by such self-examination, a bhikkhu knows:
an10.53:8.2 | S: ‘I am often content, kind-hearted, rid of dullness and drowsiness, calm, confident, loving, pure in mind, undisturbed in body, energetic, and immersed in samādhi.’ Grounded on those skillful qualities, they should practice meditation further to end the defilements.” | T: ‘I am often without longing … and concentrated,’ then he should base himself on those same wholesome qualities and make a further effort to reach the destruction of the taints.”
an10.53:8.3 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.35
sn12.35:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.35:1.2 | S: “Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “Bhikkhus, with ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.35:1.3 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.35:1.4 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates.” | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.35:1.5 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the Buddha, | T: When he had said this, a certain bhikkhu said to the Blessed One:
sn12.35:1.6 | S: “What are old age and death, sir, and who do they belong to?” | T: “Venerable sir, what now is aging-and-death, and for whom is there this aging-and-death?”
sn12.35:1.7 | S: “That’s not a cogent question,” said the Buddha. “You might say, ‘What are old age and death, and who do they belong to?’ Or you might say, ‘Old age and death are one thing, who they belong to is another.’ But both of these mean the same thing, only the phrasing differs. | T: “Not a valid question,” the Blessed One replied. “Bhikkhu, whether one says, ‘What now is aging-and-death, and for whom is there this aging-and-death?’ or whether one says, ‘Aging-and-death is one thing, the one for whom there is this aging-and-death is another’—both these assertions are identical in meaning; they differ only in the phrasing.
sn12.35:1.8 | S: Mendicant, if you have the view that the soul and the body are one and the same, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: If there is the view, ‘The soul and the body are the same,’ there is no living of the holy life;
sn12.35:1.9 | S: If you have the view that the soul is one thing, the body another, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: and if there is the view, ‘The soul is one thing, the body is another,’ there is no living of the holy life.
sn12.35:1.10 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.35:1.11 | S: ‘Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death.’” | T: ‘With birth as condition, aging-and-death. ’”
sn12.35:2.1 | S: “What is rebirth, sir, and who does it belong to?” | T: “Venerable sir, what now is birth, and for whom is there this birth?”
sn12.35:2.2 | S: “That’s not a cogent question,” said the Buddha. “You might say, ‘What is rebirth, and who does it belong to?’ Or you might say, ‘Rebirth is one thing, who it belongs to is another.’ But both of these mean the same thing, only the phrasing differs. | T: “Not a valid question,” the Blessed One replied. “Bhikkhu, whether one says, ‘What now is birth, and for whom is there this birth?’ or whether one says, ‘Birth is one thing, the one for whom there is this birth is another’—both these assertions are identical in meaning; they differ only in the phrasing….
sn12.35:2.3 | S: Mendicant, if you have the view that the soul and the body are one and the same, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: (none)
sn12.35:2.4 | S: If you have the view that the soul is one thing, the body another, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: (none)
sn12.35:2.5 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.35:2.6 | S: ‘Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth.’” | T: ‘With existence as condition, birth.’”
sn12.35:3.1 | S: “What is continued existence, sir, and who is it for?” | T: “Venerable sir, what now is existence, and for whom is there this existence?”
