sn12.35:3.3 | S: Mendicant, if you have the view that the soul and the body are one and the same, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: (none)
sn12.35:3.4 | S: If you have the view that the soul is one thing, the body another, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: (none)
sn12.35:3.5 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.35:3.6 | S: ‘Grasping is a requirement for continued existence.’ … | T: ‘With clinging as condition, existence….
sn12.35:3.7 | S: ‘Craving is a requirement for grasping.’ … | T: With craving as condition, clinging….
sn12.35:3.8 | S: ‘Feeling is a requirement for craving.’ … | T: With feeling as condition, craving….
sn12.35:3.9 | S: ‘Contact is a requirement for feeling.’ … | T: With contact as condition, feeling….
sn12.35:3.10 | S: ‘The six sense fields are requirements for contact.’ … | T: With the six sense bases as condition, contact….
sn12.35:3.11 | S: ‘Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields.’ … | T: With name-and-form as condition, the six sense bases….
sn12.35:3.12 | S: ‘Consciousness is a requirement for name and form.’ … | T: With consciousness as condition, name-and-form….
sn12.35:3.13 | S: ‘Choices are a requirement for consciousness.’” | T: With volitional formations as condition, consciousness.’”
sn12.35:4.1 | S: “What are choices, sir, and who do they belong to?” | T: “Venerable sir, what now are volitional formations, and for whom are there these volitional formations?”
sn12.35:4.2 | S: “That’s not a cogent question,” said the Buddha. “You might say, ‘What are choices, and who do they belong to?’ Or you might say, ‘Choices are one thing, who they belong to is another.’ But both of these mean the same thing, only the phrasing differs. | T: “Not a valid question,” the Blessed One replied. “Bhikkhu, whether one says, ‘What now are volitional formations, and for whom are there these volitional formations?’ or whether one says, ‘Volitional formations are one thing, the one for whom there are these volitional formations is another’—both these assertions are identical in meaning; they differ only in the phrasing.
sn12.35:4.3 | S: Mendicant, if you have the view that the soul and the body are one and the same, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: If there is the view, ‘The soul and the body are the same,’ there is no living of the holy life;
sn12.35:4.4 | S: If you have the view that the soul is one thing, the body another, there is no living of the spiritual life. | T: and if there is the view, ‘The soul is one thing, the body is another,’ there is no living of the holy life.
sn12.35:4.5 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.35:4.6 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices.’ | T: ‘With ignorance as condition, volitional formations.’
