sn11.5:14.3 | S: ‘The verses spoken by Vepacitti | T: ‘The verses spoken by Vepacitti, lord of the asuras,
sn11.5:14.4 | S: evoke punishment and violence. That’s how you get fights, arguments, and quarrels. | T: are in the sphere of punishment and violence; hence they entail conflict, contention, and strife.
sn11.5:14.5 | S: The verses spoken by Sakka | T: But the verses spoken by Sakka, lord of the devas,
sn11.5:14.6 | S: don’t evoke punishment and violence. That’s how you stay free of fights, arguments, and quarrels. | T: are in the sphere of nonpunishment and nonviolence; hence they entail freedom from conflict, freedom from contention, and freedom from strife.
sn11.5:14.7 | S: Sakka, lord of gods, wins victory by fine words!’ | T: Sakka, lord of the devas, has won the victory by well-spoken counsel.’
sn11.5:14.8 | S: And that’s how Sakka came to win victory by fine words.” | T: “In this way, bhikkhus, Sakka, lord of the devas, won the victory by well-spoken counsel.”

